WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.880
(bright music)

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:03.720
- Strongly agree.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:04.729
- Strongly disagree.

00:00:04.729 --> 00:00:05.562
- Somewhat agree.

00:00:05.562 --> 00:00:06.421
- Somewhat disagree.

00:00:06.421 --> 00:00:07.254
- I would agree.

00:00:07.254 --> 00:00:08.730
- [Both] Disagree.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.980
- I create like so many
stupid rules for myself.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.860
For college, I was like, "I
have to go drive 20 minutes

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.290
to get like a coffee,
and once I get the coffee

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.600
then I can like shift
into like focus mode,"

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.578
even though it's not
true, but it works for me.

00:00:21.578 --> 00:00:24.245
(catchy music)

00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:30.060
- [Announcer] I've
always known I have ADHD.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.493
3, 2, 1.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.240
- Yeah, in elementary
school and middle school,

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.800
I was a good student.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.450
I was quiet and I sat at my desk.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.820
In college, I started to
realize something was wrong

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.170
when I started failing classes,

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.760
and I was like, "Why am I not good enough?

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.730
Why can't I focus? Why can't
I sit in a library and study?"

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:54.420
It felt like something was
wrong versus like a diagnosis.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.060
And then, finally, last year at 28,

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.250
I finally got diagnosed,
and like every piece

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.287
kind of like came together,
and it finally felt like,

00:01:02.287 --> 00:01:04.050
"Oh, nothing's wrong with me.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.520
This is just who I am."

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.620
- So I never knew I had ADHD,

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.460
but I knew that I struggled
to keep up a lot in school,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.380
and it led to me being pretty depressed

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.120
between 15 and 16.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.557
And nothing was working
until I had one doctor say,

00:01:17.557 --> 00:01:20.430
"You know, I actually
think you have ADHD,"

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.500
and then that really just
like changed my life.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.750
- You're asking if we were aware,

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.310
and I think that's the problem with me

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.060
where I was informed at a
young age that I had ADHD,

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.660
so I didn't even understand what that was.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.220
I thought I was just like everybody else,

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.870
so I was very oblivious to it

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.250
even though I guess I was informed,

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.420
so I might need to be over there.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.690
I didn't recognize, but
I guess I'm over here.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:41.580
- I have a younger brother
who has like severe

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.740
like hyperactivity ADHD,

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.750
but for me, it was never suggested

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.090
because it wasn't like
physically manifesting itself

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.147
so nobody thought to be like,

00:01:49.147 --> 00:01:50.760
"Oh, maybe that's what's going on."

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.920
And then when I was diagnosed at like 23,

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.300
I was a little bit mad.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.030
I was like, "Why didn't
anybody like see this?"

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.500
And when I started reading about it,

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.780
that's when I found out that
like it manifests differently

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.010
in like women versus men.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.390
- When I hear that
question, it's not about me

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:04.843
understanding that I had ADHD.

00:02:04.843 --> 00:02:06.180
I think it was more the world.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.690
My depression came not because I had ADHD.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.460
It was because the schooling system

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:12.840
and the world around
me was trying to put me

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:13.800
into these boxes.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.357
So my depression came
because my teacher said,

00:02:16.357 --> 00:02:18.930
"You are gonna be a failure in life

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.090
because you're not able to do well

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.300
on this three hour standardized test."

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.960
- [Announcer] Medication has
helped me manage my ADHD.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.753
3, 2, 1.

00:02:34.764 --> 00:02:36.120
- Well, I didn't know how to answer this

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.840
because I'm not on medication
'cause I'm deathly afraid

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.180
of what it would do to me.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.380
Even though it probably would help

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.480
and I should just test it
out, I just haven't yet,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.730
and so I don't know.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.590
- I didn't know my
brain could be so quiet.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:50.737
- Yeah?
- I was like,

00:02:50.737 --> 00:02:52.770
"Wait, that's normal."

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:54.090
The medication was like putting on

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.950
a pair of glasses for the
first time in my life.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.660
I was like, "Whoa, this
is actually the world,

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.940
like, this is how people live?"

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:00.994
- All the time?

00:03:00.994 --> 00:03:03.750
- I'm like, "What the heck?
I'm so jealous of that."

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.010
- I think this is hilarious

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.890
'cause I hate meditation. (laughs)

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:10.020
But because I hated it was the reason why

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.670
I was able to end up focusing.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:14.520
I hated it so much that I was
like, "I'm just gonna focus."

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.830
I had the counter-measurements

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.780
and my mom also helped me along the way

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.950
so that I wasn't reliant on the medicine.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.660
But I still have tendencies,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.610
but I remember everything
that I was taught

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.770
and I applied them so much
to where it's kinda like

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.200
it kinda eased me off of the
medication 'cause I hated it.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.740
- Medication for me is what I used to help

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.930
get back on the horse so to speak.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.480
If things kind of spiral out of control,

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.480
try my best to basically build a house

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.640
with a strong structure.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.210
ADHD, you're building
a house on quicksand,

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:51.450
so sometimes the medication
hardens that quicksand

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:52.500
for me a little bit.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.930
Yeah, that's my experience
with medication.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.960
- I was diagnosed later
on at 27 years old,

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.150
and so the first time I took medication,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.327
I finally realized like

00:04:01.327 --> 00:04:03.660
"Oh, this is what it feels like

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.750
to not get brain fog after 20 minutes."

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:08.910
I said agree because
I've been able to learn

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:11.310
certain things and how to
navigate without medication.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:16.080
I do much more meditation
to slow down my brain.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:17.850
Now, the reason why
that's beneficial for me

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:22.410
is that would work even
if I'm not on medicine.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:26.159
- [Announcer] My family
helped seek out my diagnosis.

00:04:26.159 --> 00:04:31.159
3, 2, 1.
(participants giggling)

00:04:34.272 --> 00:04:37.260
- My mom definitely takes
like mental health serious.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.080
One time she told me that I
don't have personal space.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.390
I didn't know what that was,
so maybe that's the reason why

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:44.610
she got me diagnosed very early.

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:45.960
- My parents are a little older,

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.250
they had me when they were older,

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.156
so I like to say that
they tried their best,

00:04:49.156 --> 00:04:51.454
but they maybe didn't have the tools

00:04:51.454 --> 00:04:53.160
to help me when I was younger.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.560
It wasn't until college when
my grades started dropping

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.690
that they started to notice
that something might be wrong.

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:01.290
When I finally got
diagnosed and I told my mom,

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:02.857
she was very supportive and she was like,

00:05:02.857 --> 00:05:04.890
"Can I do anything? Can
I help with anything?"

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.380
I think it's just having the
tools available for parents

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.900
to know kind of what the signs are

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.250
even if they're not super apparent.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.970
- For me, my parents still don't think...

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:19.200
My dad was in a civil war in Nigeria.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.140
He has a PhD. I have three older brothers.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:24.870
They had all As, merit, scholarship,

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:26.370
and my parents were like, "Yes!"

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:27.203
And I'm a triplet,

00:05:27.203 --> 00:05:29.340
so I have a twin brother
and a twin sister.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.340
All three of us, we just,

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:33.810
we're not very good in school.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.060
What was lucky was that we all ended up

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:37.860
getting sports scholarships.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:41.040
So my parents were able
to find success in me,

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.590
not through academics, but through sports.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:44.887
But they would still say today,

00:05:44.887 --> 00:05:47.880
"No, just work harder. Just focus.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.130
You just need to make sure
you're not on social media

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:51.180
and have your phone next to you."

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.400
- Similar to you, I'm Vietnamese,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.470
so older generations than me

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.110
they've gone through war periods, right?

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.570
So to them it's, "We've survived a war.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.870
What's this issue about you
not being able to focus,"

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:07.140
in fourth grade math class, for example.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.780
But over time, we seeked help together,

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:14.070
and then actually I found
out my father has ADHD too.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:18.243
He just floated through life
managing it his own way.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:21.090
- [Announcer] I have developed mechanisms

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.420
to cope with or manage my ADHD symptoms.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.153
3, 2, 1.

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:32.730
- Especially with school, we went online

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:34.230
to like Zoom university,

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:37.320
like the last year of
my college education,

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.360
and I was like, "There's
no way this is happening."

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.210
And so I was like, "I have
to invent some sort of system

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.820
for this to work, or else I
will literally not graduate."

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:47.100
I create like so many
stupid rules for myself,

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.510
but it works for me.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:50.107
And so like for college, I was like,

00:06:50.107 --> 00:06:53.160
"I have to go drive 20
minutes to get like a coffee,

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:54.330
and once I get the coffee,

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:56.610
then I can shift into like focus mode,

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:57.840
even though that's not true."

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.450
But I tricked myself, I Pavloved myself

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:01.470
into thinking that way.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:04.417
It works, and I feel like I'm
so stubborn that I'm like,

00:07:04.417 --> 00:07:05.970
"I'm just gonna live my life with these

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.700
like stupid little rules
because that's what helps me,

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:09.990
and I don't care if that's not the way

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:12.810
that like other people
do it, it works for me."

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.390
- Zoom university was a struggle at first

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:16.650
just trying to figure it out.

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:17.550
I actually have the same,

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:20.370
I have like little morning
rituals where I would wake up,

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:23.010
make myself a tea, and
just spend 20 minutes

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.120
looking at the plants,

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.888
and also I found sticky
notes to be my best friend

00:07:26.888 --> 00:07:29.160
and my enemy at the same time

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:32.340
because sometimes those
sticky notes pile up.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.960
- Yeah, I also have just like,

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:37.140
I live and die by my
calendar on my fridge.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:38.100
If it's not on there,

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:39.960
I'm never going to your birthday party.

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:42.480
It has to be on the
fridge on the calendar.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.460
That's not good 'cause
then if I'm on vacation

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.230
or I'm not able to look at the calendar,

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:47.400
or I forget something,

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.860
then it's just gone
outta my brain forever.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:51.330
- I call it like the light bulb trick

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:52.797
where if I wanna study,

00:07:52.797 --> 00:07:55.800
I have a specific lamp that I turn on,

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.650
and every time it's on, I
just know this is focus mode.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.840
I don't do anything else.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.580
- [Announcer] ADHD is
becoming more common.

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:04.413
3, 2, 1.

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:14.975
- I am seeing like apps like TikTok,

00:08:14.975 --> 00:08:16.770
everything's getting very shorter.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:19.410
You're being shown like so
much information at once,

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.210
and like your attention span is obviously

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:24.900
gonna like get shorter as
you're exposed to that.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.880
- I think ADHD has always been there.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:29.190
On social media, especially
people are starting

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:31.170
to create more of a safer environment

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:32.490
to talk about mental health.

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:37.200
That awareness has brought
more people getting diagnosed,

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.677
and because of that
people might think that,

00:08:38.677 --> 00:08:41.070
"Oh yeah, everyone has ADHD."

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:42.480
- And now, they have some
people on social media

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.760
being like, "Signs that
you might have ADHD,"

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:46.417
and they're so much more than just like,

00:08:46.417 --> 00:08:47.460
"I can't sit still."

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:48.870
It's like so many other things,

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:51.270
so more people are like,
"That might be me too."

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:53.100
Women are getting diagnosed more now,

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.320
like they're listening to us,
like doctors are listening,

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:56.970
and they're not like, "Oh,
they're just like crazy women."

00:08:56.970 --> 00:08:59.880
Now, it's like, "Oh,
there's something going on.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:00.713
It might be ADHD."

00:09:00.713 --> 00:09:03.300
- When I first started going to therapy,

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:04.950
one of the first questions is

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.870
do you either work on Wall Street

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:10.380
or are you a college student?

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:12.990
I said, "No, I'm dealing with some issues,

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:16.177
I'd like to get diagnosed."

00:09:16.177 --> 00:09:17.460
"Well, we don't do that here.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:18.967
Tell me some of your symptoms."

00:09:18.967 --> 00:09:21.090
"Oh, you have lack of focus.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:23.640
Let's start off with 25
milligrams in Adderall."

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:25.260
It's like a revolving door.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:26.940
Outside of the waiting room,

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:28.770
there's a bunch of other college kids

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:31.470
waiting to get their Adderall also.

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:34.320
- How most people are tested for ADHD

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:35.730
isn't through a brain scan.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.910
So it's through questions and symptoms

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:41.580
that anybody can necessarily make up.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.740
In addition, if you even look at

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.540
how marketing has been playing a role,

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:48.720
is our attention span is
getting less and less and less.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:50.430
And so I do believe because of that,

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.430
it will cause more people
to identify as ADHD.

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:55.260
- I agree with that, and I realized

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:56.760
I think I'm on the wrong side.

00:09:57.795 --> 00:09:58.937
I think this is why

00:09:58.937 --> 00:10:00.540
'cause I was like, "Yeah, yeah, yeah,

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:01.530
you're making a lot of sense."

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:03.000
I strongly agree,

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.620
and I one-up everything
that you just said.

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:07.140
- We have information thrown at us 24/7.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:09.420
I don't go on TikTok anymore,
but when I'd go on TikTok,

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:12.990
I would get advertisements
for like ADHD medications,

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:15.810
like no doctor needed,
and I was like "What?"

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.750
- They're marketed towards
like young women too.

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:20.850
There was a whole like
study done about it.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:22.260
- Yeah, I almost feel like they're trying

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:25.200
to profit off of people when in reality

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:27.270
I think it's more of a societal issue

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:29.400
of like society is just trying to push us

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:33.123
to be more productive past
what is humanly possible.

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:35.670
- [Announcer] ADHD will prevent me

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:38.610
from accomplishing certain
goals in the future.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:41.253
3, 2, 1.

00:10:50.173 --> 00:10:53.130
- I hope not.
(participants laughing)

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:55.800
- I think if you had asked
me two or three years ago,

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:57.467
I would've been like strongly agree

00:10:57.467 --> 00:11:01.260
'cause I was like "Why
can I not do X, Y, and Z?

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:03.060
And that's frustrating,
and I think what's great

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:05.460
about getting like
professionally diagnosed

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:06.900
and like kind of pointers,

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:09.450
I'm like, "Oh, I don't
have to do those things

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:10.500
that I'm not good at."

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:12.480
It's a lot better of a mindset than having

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:13.957
like a very low self-esteem being like,

00:11:13.957 --> 00:11:15.990
"Why can I do this? Why can't I do this?"

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:17.580
It's like, "I don't have to."

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:20.190
- No matter what circumstance I'm in

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:21.330
or what I've gone through,

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:22.890
I'm gonna figure out a
way how to make it out.

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:24.810
Maybe I'm just hard on myself,

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:27.150
and if I didn't do well,
I didn't blame it on ADHD.

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:28.530
I just thought I didn't figure it out

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:30.090
or I didn't try hard enough.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:31.260
There's something else I could have done.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:35.130
- I'm fortunate to have a lot of support

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:37.774
and different resources to help me

00:11:37.774 --> 00:11:40.470
go through the ADHD journey.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:43.410
You know, ADHD doesn't stop. It's 24/7.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.540
- If you asked me when
I was in high school,

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:48.690
I'm like 100% it was gonna affect me.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:51.540
Now, as I'm almost 40,
you know, I was lucky

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:53.730
to be a, you know, professional athlete,

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:55.020
I'm lucky to travel all over the world,

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:56.880
work at, you know, some amazing companies,

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:58.890
but my goal is no longer for me.

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:00.540
I have a 10 year old daughter

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:02.640
that's suffering from the same,

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.420
and I see some of the same
stuff that happened to me

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:07.860
to her in school.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:10.710
And so my focus now isn't
towards me professionally,

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:13.020
I feel good, but my goal
now is how do I make sure

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:17.520
that my daughter is able
to navigate effectively

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:19.740
through the same stuff
that I went through?

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:21.360
She will be fine, but
I just wanna make sure

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:23.370
that she's able to navigate that through

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:24.570
her schooling career.

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:27.051
- [Announcer] Time to
wrap up this episode.

00:12:27.051 --> 00:12:28.545
Thank you, everybody.

00:12:28.545 --> 00:12:31.295
(group clapping)

00:12:34.904 --> 00:12:37.571
(catchy music)

